natural_abilities:
  - name: "Spell Casting Through Devices"
    description: "As the techno-wizard: spells are cast through an appropriate mechanical device used as the focus, such as a pistol to fire a Fire Bolt or goggles to See the Invisible. The device is not harmed and is no more magical afterwards; only full techno-wizard conversion makes a device magic. Device and spell cannot both be used at once. Casting by words and gestures alone halves all spell ranges, durations, M.D.C./S.D.C. and damage."
  - name: "Techno-Wizard Devices"
    description: "Trained in combining magic and machines to build techno-wizard devices, powered by the user''s P.P.E. or I.S.P. and directed by thought. The clan''s TW ninja are known for armor enchanted with Invisibility: Superior and for converted weapons."
  - name: "Psionics of the Techno-Wizard"
    description: "Identical to the regular techno-wizard: a minor psychic with Machine Ghost, Mind Block, Object Read, Speed Reading, Telemechanics and Total Recall. I.S.P.: 4D6 plus M.E., +1D4+1 per level."
  - name: "Initial Spell Knowledge"
    description: "Knows the techno-wizard''s level one spells (Blinding Flash, Globe of Daylight, See the Invisible, Sense Magic), and only two more spells, from level three. The spell casting penalties are the techno-wizard''s."
  - name: "Learning New Spells"
    description: "Other spells can be purchased or learned, but only the clan teaches magic in Japan and it decides which spells come next; the G.M. chooses them. Learning spells anywhere else without the clan''s express consent is treason, punishable by death."
  - name: "P.P.E."
    description: "A living battery of mystic energy. Permanent Base P.P.E.: 1D6x10+10 plus the P.E. attribute, +2D6 per additional level of experience."
restrictions:
  - "O.C.C. bonuses beyond the modeled saves: +1 to Spell Strength at levels 5, 9 and 13."
  - "Spells: only those the clan chooses to teach. Seeking spells elsewhere without the clan''s consent is treason, punishable by death."
  - "Cybernetics: starts with cyber-armor and 1D4 implants of choice, plus the clan''s tracer chip. Additional implants must be bought by the character."
side_effects: "Also starts with 4D4x1000 credits'' worth of black market salable items. A freelance spy or assassin of good reputation at 4th level or higher can charge 4000 to 10,000 credits for a single day''s operation."
extraction_notes: "Rifts World Book 8: Japan printed 89-91 (cache p090-p092, text layer, page_offset +1). The entry opens under the Ninja Techno-Wizard O.C.C. heading at the foot of printed 89 (right column), gives the Powers of the Ninja Techno-Wizard on printed 90 left and the stat block from printed 90 right to the Money and Cybernetics lines at the top of printed 91; the SAMAS Samurai Pilot follows. Pages 90 and 91 read from renders; they match the text layer. || GROUP: filed among the Republic of Japan O.C.C.s, which name no group; stored as magic with men_of_arms false, as the techno-wizard is, since it is a spell caster. || XP: the Japanese Imp, Ninja Techno-Wizard column of the Experience Point Tables (printed 216, cache p217), read off a render; it differs from the RUE techno-wizard''s ladder from level 9 up. || POWERS: the book says the powers are the Rifts RPG techno-wizard''s (the original core book, printed 89-90) unless noted. That book is not in the cache; the class is modelled on the catalog''s techno-wizard (RUE p.126-129). Psionics are copied from it (identical per the book). The book restates Magic Bonuses in full, so the RUE techno-wizard''s +2 vs possession and mind control are NOT carried. +1 save vs magic at levels 4, 7, 11 and 14 is stored as spell_magic and ritual_magic, as the techno-wizard stores its save vs magic. Spell Strength has no key and is in restrictions. The RUE Techno-Wizardry Construction skill and Ley Line Piloting are not granted: the book lists neither, and both are RUE''s. || SPELLS: the book says all level one spells are unchanged and only two more spells are learned at level three. Read as: the four level-one spells of the techno-wizard''s list (catalog level 1: Blinding Flash, Globe of Daylight, See the Invisible, Sense Magic) are granted, plus two picks from the eight level-three spells on that list. The original core book''s level-by-level starting list could not be checked; the level-two Cloak of Darkness is not granted under this reading. A judgment call. No spells per level: new spells come only from the clan, at the G.M.''s choice. || SKILLS: Japanese is Language: Native Tongue at the catalog''s 98% (the printed +20% cannot raise it) and Literacy: Native Language 40+15. Other bases are catalog base plus the printed bonus. Computer Repair prints +10%) with a lost parenthesis. Criminal sciences and forensics is the catalog''s Forensics. Electrical and Mechanical picks count as two selections each, which the app cannot charge; it is in the related note. Secondary skills: two at each of levels 1, 4, 8 and 12, limited as the related list, no bonuses. || HAND TO HAND: Expert; Assassin, Martial Arts or Aikido for one other skill, Karate or Jujitsu for two; no alignment condition is printed. || EQUIPMENT: the book prints two weapon lines, the TW line (one converted energy weapon, an additional energy pistol, 1D4 E-clips) and a second line repeated from the Ninja Borg (one energy weapon of choice, 4 E-clips, one vibro-blade, one ancient weapon); both are stored. The armor''s two TW features are in the armor''s note. The micro-film camera and small crowbar are this book''s micro-film-camera and small-crowbar rows (printed 83). Money: 3D6x1000 credits; 4D4x1000 in black market items is in side_effects. Cybernetics: cyber-armor and the tracer chip as gear, 1D4 implants of choice in restrictions."
---

## Lore

Techno-wizardry is almost unknown in Japan. Sorcery and science distrust and even hate each other on the islands, and most authorities frown on any alliance between them. Only the ninja, who seldom work within the law and care little for approval, have dared to meddle in the field, and so the tech-ninja clans hold a monopoly on it.

It began in the Otomo Shogunate, after a band of mystic ninja ambushed and wiped out a tech-ninja team of juicers and ''borgs. Seeking a way to match the mystic ninja, the clan tied to H-Brand set its scientists to work with a group of Otomo sorcerers, and in 99 P.A. they developed techno-wizardry on their own, apart from similar discoveries elsewhere. Armor enchanted with superior invisibility let TW ninja carry out missions nobody else could, and in a later clash with the mystic ninja clan their devices turned the tide.

Fewer than one in a hundred tech-ninja ever learn the art. The few with an aptitude for magic are trained in magic and assassination at once, under the harshest regime in the ninja camps. The result is a handful of highly accomplished magicians who are worked hard, seldom allowed any adventure, and often bitter about it; some of them plot to leave, or even betray, the clan.

## Equipment and Money

The personalized body armor is usually IA-100 Infiltrator Armor and may carry two techno-wizard features of the character''s choice, Invisibility included but never Impervious to Energy. The converted energy weapon runs on P.P.E. rather than normal energy. The G.M. decides whether the character starts with any further specialized ninja equipment. Money: 3D6x1000 credits and 4D4x1000 credits'' worth of black market salable items.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'ninja-techno-wizard');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'ninja-techno-wizard';

